    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c6fd164e-86c9-5bb8-8f9f-a7d4a125c9d2",
      ":question/problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u\u22a5v$ and $a<0$, find the value of $a.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $u\u22a5v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  & =a\\cdot a+2\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ {a}^{2}-14 & =0 \\\\ {a}^{2} & =14 \\\\ a & =\\pm \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $a<0.$ Therefore, $a=-\\sqrt{14}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
