    "question": {
      ":question/id": "95fd0f63-500b-53e8-80c8-0ca801ae9809",
      ":question/problem": "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4x\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u\u22a5v$ and $x>0$, find the value of $x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $u\u22a5v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)+4x\\cdot x \\\\  & =-12+4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -12+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $x>0.$ Therefore, $x=\\sqrt{3}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
